Time Complexity: O(n+m), where n is the number of courses and m is the size of prerequisite array .Auxiliary Space: O(n+m)[Approach 2]  Using DFS - O(m+n) Time and O(m+n) SpaceWe can use DFS for topological sorting by treating courses as nodes and prerequisites as edges (b → a). DFS visits all prerequisites before a course, ensuring dependencies come first. By tracking visiting nodes, we can detect cycles. Adding courses to a stack after visiting dependencies and then reversing it gives a valid course order.
